<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Ocean Life and Conservation – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <h3 style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em; font-style: italic;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</h3>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Ocean Life and Conservation</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Plants That Live in the Ocean</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Reading: Outline the main information from simple diagrams (e.g., graphs, bar charts). Reading: Comprehend descriptive texts about places or events.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can outline the main points from bar charts about marine biodiversity in Panama by identifying key species and trends (e.g., "List the two most common species found in Coiba National Park's coral reefs." "Summarize the percentage of mangrove coverage shown in the chart for the Gulf of Montijo." "Identify changes in fish populations in the Bay of Panama over time based on the bar chart.")
Can comprehend descriptive texts about ocean ecosystems in Panama by identifying key characteristics and ecological importance (e.g., "Describe the coral formations and marine species found in Isla Iguanas reef." "Explain why the Bocas del Toro archipelago is known for its seagrass beds and dolphin populations." "List the unique species that thrive in the Coiba National Park marine reserve.")
Can outline trends from graphs about ocean pollution levels (e.g., "The graph shows that plastic waste in the ocean has increased over the past five years.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images showing various plants that live in the ocean (e.g., different types of seaweed, seagrass, mangroves, plankton visible under a microscope). Students are asked to identify what they see and share anything they know about these 'ocean plants'. The teacher prompts using the present simple: "What do you see in the pictures? Where do these plants live? Why are they important for the ocean ecosystem?" (3-5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to quickly scan a short text for key information and how to interpret a simple bar chart. For instance, the teacher points to a simple bar chart (e.g., "Mangrove Coverage in Panama") and says, "Look, this bar is for Bocas del Toro. It shows 70% coverage. This is how we find information quickly." The teacher also models the pronunciation of key vocabulary from the previous lesson, focusing on stress in "ecosystem," "biodiversity," and minimal pairs like "reef" /iː/ and "riff" /ɪ/ using large visual aids. (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are we going to read slowly for every single word, or quickly for the main idea?" (To check understanding of scanning)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I ask you to identify the main point of a graph, are you looking for a small detail or the overall trend?" (To check understanding of outlining main information)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(2 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10-12 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a short descriptive text (narrative excerpt, A2.4 level) titled "Panama's Green Ocean Gardens: Mangroves and Seagrass" focusing on the characteristics and ecological importance of these plants in Panamanian coastal areas. The text includes several target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>mangroves, seagrass, habitat, ecosystem, biodiversity, conservation, coastal areas, protect</i>). Alongside the text, there is a simple bar chart showing "Percentage of Mangrove Coverage by Region in Panama" or a line graph illustrating "Changes in Seagrass Bed Area over 10 Years in Bocas del Toro."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student comprehends descriptive texts about ocean ecosystems in Panama by identifying key characteristics and ecological importance. The student outlines the main information from simple diagrams (e.g., graphs, bar charts).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the text quickly and answer: "What is the main topic of this text?" and "What kinds of ocean plants does it describe?" (2 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Questions (Text):</b> Students read the text more carefully to answer 3-4 True/False questions based on key facts about mangroves and seagrass (e.g., "Mangroves protect the shoreline from waves." "Seagrass beds are important habitats for fish species."). (3 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Diagram):</b> Students look at the bar chart/graph and match regions or years with their corresponding percentages/trends. E.g., "Match each region with its mangrove coverage percentage from the chart." "Does the graph show that seagrass area is increasing or decreasing?" (3 min)</li>
                </ol>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(8 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(8 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Present simple for describing ocean life ("Mangroves are homes for many species."), present continuous for ongoing actions ("Plastic waste is polluting our oceans."), modals for suggestions and obligations ("We should protect our coastal areas.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> ocean, plants, species, coral reefs, marine life, ecosystem, pollution, habitat, biodiversity, fish, shoreline, seaweed, plankton, conservation, coastal areas, mangroves, protected zones, protect, conserve, preserve, sustainable, fragile.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Stress on "ecosystem," "biodiversity," "fragile."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Vocabulary Cloze):</b> Students work individually to complete a cloze activity with 5-6 sentences extracted from the text, using a word bank of target vocabulary (e.g., "Mangroves provide a rich ______ for various marine species." <i>(habitat)</i> "The ________ of our oceans is vital for future generations." <i>(conservation)</i>). (4 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Rephrasing & Graph Interpretation):</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students rephrase sentences from the text using specified grammar points. E.g., "The text says people throw trash in the ocean." (rephrase using a modal obligation: "We should not pollute the ocean.") "The graph shows a rise in temperature." (rephrase using present continuous: "Ocean temperatures are rising.") (3 min)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students complete sentences based on the bar chart/graph. E.g., "According to the chart, the region with the most mangroves ______ (is/has) ______% coverage." (3 min)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Peer Check):</b> Students compare their answers from the cloze and sentence completion activities. They discuss any discrepancies and justify their choices using evidence from the text or diagram. The teacher circulates, provides feedback, and monitors pronunciation, specifically stressing multisyllabic words. (4 min)</li>
                </ol>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(14 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(14 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Reading and Outline Creation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a new, short descriptive text (A2.4 level) about another specific Panamanian ocean plant (e.g., "The Vital Role of Plankton in Panama's Ocean Ecosystem" or "Unique Seaweed Species of Panama") and an accompanying simple bar chart or graph showing related data (e.g., "Plankton Population Trends in the Gulf of Panama" or "Diversity of Seaweed Species in Coiba National Park").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group reads their assigned text and analyzes the diagram.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Their task is to collaboratively create a short outline (bullet points) that:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies the key characteristics and ecological importance of the ocean plant described in the text (e.g., "Plankton are tiny organisms. They are the base of the marine food chain. Many fish species depend on them.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Summarizes the main information and trends from the diagram (e.g., "The graph shows that plankton populations are decreasing since 2020. This is a problem for the fish.").</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups prepare to share their outline orally with the class in a brief, clear presentation. (e.g., "We should protect these tiny plants because they are crucial.")</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in identifying information from texts and diagrams, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion. Students are encouraged to use present simple to describe facts and present continuous for trends, and appropriate vocabulary (<i>species, ecosystem, fragile, conserve</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly presents their outline from Stage 4. The teacher observes their ability to clearly communicate the main points from the text and the trends from the diagram.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to identify and outline main points from descriptive texts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in summarizing trends from bar charts/graphs.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>ecosystem, biodiversity, habitat, conserve, sustain</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and intelligibility of pronunciation, especially for multisyllabic words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Evidence of collaborative interaction within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one piece of information you found challenging to outline from the diagram today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List two new vocabulary words you practiced reading today."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did I understand the texts and diagrams about ocean plants today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What reading strategies (e.g., scanning, outlining) helped me the most to find the main information?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students outline information from texts and diagrams? (e.g., the visual aids, guided questions for the charts, pair work for discussion).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face with the reading tasks or vocabulary? Were specific grammar points (e.g., present continuous for trends) difficult for them to identify?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Speaking & Interaction) to ensure continued vocabulary use and correct grammar application? (e.g., provide sentence starters for discussions, a quick review of conservation action vocabulary).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "In our next lesson, we will use this vocabulary and knowledge about ocean plants and their importance to discuss and propose specific conservation actions. We will actively speak and interact using these ideas."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(3 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(3 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>